A||||9||Pretender||why no headder wrap?||||5||
Z||000000||Pretender||03-08-2000||03:16 PM||||when i orderd my headders phill said that i shouldn't wrap them because it will cause breakage. Why? I thought that wrapping kept the headders cool and engine heat down.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||1FST2M6||03-08-2000||04:07 PM||||Breakage of what? I've got my stock manifolds wraped with caramic tape from Thermo-TEC. I've wrapped my girlfriends Caramic coated SPS header (Saturn Performance Systems) with the same material I figues 3 coats of caramic was better than 2... and the Stainless ones (factory installed headders) on my Alfa 164L are also wraped.. Should have bought stock in Thermo-TEC...  What did he say would break? All of my pieces have been wraped for WELL over a year and at least 12k miles on each and not a bit of trouble! ||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroMeister||03-08-2000||04:16 PM||||Can't say for sure if this is correct but it is my understanding based on info that I have been given.  The wrap actually keeps the heat in the pipes and they overheat and the metal fatigues and begins to deteriorate.  This helps keep the engine compartment temps down but is hell on the pipes.  Once you get a little away from the heads the pipes are cool enough that it is OK to wrap.  Again, just my take on info that I have.<p>[This message has been edited by FieroMeister (edited 03-08-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000003||theogre||03-08-2000||06:48 PM||||"Header wrap" keeps heat in the manifold/header it's applied to. It is usually not desired or needed on a street car. For one thing it can make the header run so hot that the metal crystalizes and fails.<P>On Fiero V6, it could be considered dangerous. If any of the conditions occure that would normally make the manifold glow, with wrap on it will likely melt. (Glowing manifolds are a common problem. It's easy to have it happen.)<P>The wrap is only needed in a few specific cases. Like when you have to keep heat away from other areas and a deflector sheild isn't enough.||||reg||1
Z||000004||1FST2M6||03-08-2000||11:03 PM||||Guess I've just been very fortunate and have had no problems. I've been tols by all the guys at the Speed Shop "..Keep heat in the pipes..." and not to let it bleed into the other systems of the car... (eg. intake, cooling etc.) ||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||03-09-2000||11:40 AM||||If there is proper air flow thru the engine compartment bleed heat isn't an issue. You can use a couple small aluminum shields to protect other things, like starters, and more often than not actually get better protection.<P>My 80 pinto for example packed the exhaust, battery, and starter, into about 3 cubic feet of space. There where only a couple inches at most between parts. All it ever had were 2 tin shields one for the battery and one for the starter. It never had any problems at all. That right side of the motor pumped out alot of heat.<P>You have to keep in mind that there are 3 methods of transmitting heat in a car.<P>air convection. Convection normally wants to go no place but up. deflecting convection away, like Fiero's battery shield, will efectivly eliminate heating of a part. An intake manifold on the other side of the block is not going to get any heating by convection from the exhaust. (With the rare exception of Fiero where the muffler is under the intake.)<P>contucted thru parts. you either have to move the parts or create barriers.<P>Infra red. Any thing that will block/reflect the IR will protect the parts. could be a peice of aluminum foil and be 100% effective. This is why the microscopic layer of reflectant on some high performance plug wires works so well.<P>Some people are likely to claim dyno results prove the wrap works...... question is did they open the hood durring the baseling run? if they didn't then all the convected heat had no place to go. On a dyno there is no air rammed thru and around the car to carry it away or to blow on IR exposed parts. This makes a big diferance in how things work.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 03-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||1FST2M6||03-09-2000||01:29 PM||||I'm almost 100% sure i didn't see any gain on my HP or torque numbers cause of this mod... but it was piece of mind just knowing that if i did ever have a wire or somthing come in contact with my exposed exhaust pieces they wouldn't die right away. The power increase wasn't really what I was looking for... it would help, but since i yanked all my shields off i needed some kind of protection and if this protection helped performance thats great... One of the largest engine bay temp decreasing procedures I did was just unscrewing the bottom of the vents @ the rear window! and that was FREE!   Sorry for getting off the subject... I still don't get why you shouldn't wrap the other hedders? If theres a Ceramic coating on them you shouldn't have to worry about deterioration due to the increased heat... The shop i deal with ceramic coats EVERY piece of their upgraded turbo kits for various cars (Starions, Supras, Civics etc.) and always wraps the down pipe with Thermo-TEC ceramic tape, all the way to the cat. Their is WAY more heat in a turbo car than our naturaly aspirated cars and I'm not aware of anyone having problems with thier pipes. I guess i'm missing somthing here... are the hedders just not very thick and prone to breaking anyway... like a stock manifold or??? enlighten me please.. I'm on the same track of buying hedders too and don't want to waste my money if their not built well... ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cooter||03-09-2000||03:28 PM||||Another problem with the wrap is that it limits thermal expansion where the bands or clamps are used to hold it in place. <BR>I had the header on my Civic wrapped in steam pipe insulation- like the thermo-tech only much cheaper (it was free from a friend) and thicker. I finally got tired of the annoying noise the car made with the header and removed it. Where the clamps were, you could see the pipe was smaller than the rest of the tube. When the pipe heated up, it expanded, but the clamps did not. If I had ran the headder much longer, it probably would have broke on every pipe.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Pretender||03-10-2000||12:15 AM||||My last car was an '89 conquest and i had the pipes wrapped from the turbo back, God i loved that car. 13.0's with just a few extra goodies i installed.||||reg||4
Z||000009||1FST2M6||03-10-2000||02:25 PM||||Conquests are SWEET and STUPID fast with no real work!!!  <A HREF="http://www.engineered.net" TARGET=_blank>www.engineered.net</A>    see the starion/conquest dyno day results! impressive!||||reg||1
